## SECTION 5 — AUDIENCE INTELLIGENCE
**who_they_are**: First-time or infrequent DIYers, mostly 18-30, who have committed to attending a festival and have decided (often for budget reasons) to do their own nails rather than book a salon appointment. Some own basic polish only; a smaller segment owns a gel kit but is unsure how to apply festival-specific designs with it.

**what_they_want_from_this_post**: A genuinely followable, linear process — not a roundup of pretty photos with a tutorial buried at the bottom. They want to know exactly what to buy, what they can substitute, and how long it will take.

**what_frustrates_them**: Existing tutorials assume a full salon-grade kit (UV lamp, dip powder, French molds); Quora threads reveal real anxiety about "messing it up" and doing damage (acrylic threads dominated by warnings and horror stories); most YouTube/blog content shows the finished look without breaking down beginner failure points (uneven lines, chipping, thick coats).

**top_5_pain_points**:
1. Don't own a UV/LED lamp and assume that rules out gel-look festival nails entirely
2. Fear of wasting money on a kit they'll only use once
3. Uncertain how long realistic designs actually take (worried it eats their whole prep day)
4. Anxiety about mess — glitter and polish everywhere, cleanup dread
5. Don't know which designs actually survive dancing/heat/crowds vs. which are Pinterest-photo-only

**exact_user_phrases**: "how to do own nails for the very first time," "salon-quality nails from the comfort of your home," "no salon needed," "beginner-friendly, homeowner-tested," "don't need a salon to make it happen," "surprisingly easy to DIY, even from your kitchen table," "thin layers are always better than thick ones," "practice, practice, practice," "it's trickier than it looks," "my apartment looked like a bomb went off, glitter EVERYWHERE"

**intent_content_signals**: Numbered/sequential step format outperforms freeform description; specific time estimates per design build trust; budget/shopping-list framing reduces purchase anxiety; explicit "no lamp needed" callouts perform well on TikTok; encouragement/reassurance language ("your first try doesn't have to be perfect") matches tone gap in Quora threads.

